## Final Judgement
## Step 1: Key Insights from the Fact-Check

The evidence establishes that the U.S. GDP grew at an annualized rate of 33.1% in Q3 2020, which was indeed the largest quarterly GDP growth on record (with quarterly data going back to 1947). The previous record was 3.9% in Q1 1950. Numerically, 33.1% is approximately 8.5 times larger than the previous record of 3.9%—not "almost triple" as Trump claimed. Additionally, the 33.1% figure was an annualized projection (the actual quarterly growth was 7.4%), and it represented a rebound from an unprecedented 31.4% annualized contraction in Q2 2020 caused by COVID-19 lockdowns.

## Step 2: Remaining Information

The core factual questions have been answered. The remaining question is interpretive: how to characterize the overall claim given that the main factual assertions are correct, but the "almost triple" characterization is an understatement, and the claim omits critical context about the rebound nature of the growth.

## Step 3: Conclusion

The core claim that 33.1% was "the biggest in the history of our country" is technically true and supported by the evidence. However, the claim cherry-picks by (1) omitting the critical context that this was a rebound from a record 31.4% contraction in the previous quarter, (2) using the annualized rate (33.1%) rather than the actual quarterly rate (7.4%), and (3) characterizing the magnitude as "almost triple" the previous record when it was actually more than 8 times larger. Including the context of the prior quarter's historic decline, the fact that the economy was still below pre-pandemic levels, and the proper magnitude comparison would create a significantly different impression than the triumphant framing Trump presented.

`cherry-picking`

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim that the U.S. GDP grew at 33.1%—the "biggest in the history of our country"—is factually accurate: the Commerce Department reported a record annualized quarterly GDP growth rate of 33.1% for Q3 2020, the highest since quarterly data began being tracked in 1947, surpassing the previous record of 3.9% set in Q1 1950 ([CBS News](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/gdp-q3-growth-33-1-percent/), [Al Jazeera](https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2020/10/29/us-economy-turns-in-record-q3-growth-but-crisis-is-not-over), [USAFacts](https://usafacts.org/articles/gdp-grew-74-third-quarter-2020-remains-lower-it-was-pre-pandemic-levels/)). However, the claim cherry-picks by omitting critical context: the 33.1% figure is an annualized projection (the actual quarter-over-quarter growth was 7.4%), and it represented a rebound from an unprecedented 31.4% annualized contraction in Q2 2020 caused by COVID-19 lockdowns ([CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/29/economy/gdp-report-trump-biggest-best-factcheck/index.html)). Furthermore, Trump's characterization that 33.1% was "almost triple" the previous record is a significant understatement—33.1% is actually more than eight times larger than the prior record of 3.9% ([USAFacts](https://usafacts.org/articles/gdp-grew-74-third-quarter-2020-remains-lower-it-was-pre-pandemic-levels/)). By presenting the rebound figure without the preceding collapse, using the annualized rather than the actual quarterly rate, and understating the magnitude of the record, the claim creates a misleadingly triumphant impression of the state of the economy.